<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(LibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!--
      DatabentoDotNet.* throughout — package ID, assembly, and namespace. Never Databento.*:
      that is the vendor's namespace and an unreserved NuGet prefix they could claim at any
      time. See CLAUDE.md, "Naming".
    -->
    <PackageId>DatabentoDotNet.Reference</PackageId>
    <AssemblyName>DatabentoDotNet.Reference</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>DatabentoDotNet.Reference</RootNamespace>
    <Description>Databento reference data client for .NET: security master, corporate actions, and adjustment factors.</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    ILogger for ReferenceClient.LoggerFactory. It reaches this project transitively through
    DatabentoDotNet.Historical already; it is named here because it also appears in this project's
    own public API — the same reason DatabentoDotNet.Live names NodaTime.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.SourceLink.GitHub" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The transport, by project reference (#48).

    Upstream's reference client is a separate type in the same crate and reaches the historical
    transport through crate-internal visibility: reference.rs:15 imports
    historical::{AddToForm, HistoricalGateway, ReqwestForm, API_VERSION}, and its subclients call
    historical::{handle_response, handle_zstd_jsonl_response}. Its own `request` (reference.rs:81-91)
    composes `v{API_VERSION}/{slug}` against a base URL derived from the *historical* gateway and
    attaches the same HTTP Basic credential, with the same `Accept: application/json` default
    header and the same user agent. The reference API is the historical transport with a different
    set of slugs, and upstream says so by construction.

    Separate .NET assemblies have no equivalent of `pub(crate)`, and this repo declares no
    InternalsVisibleTo anywhere — deliberately, and this is not the place to spend the first one.
    What is left is the honest option: HistoricalClient's transport is public on purpose (see its
    remarks, HistoricalClient.cs:60-68), so this package uses the public API rather than a private
    channel into a sibling. No new transport code is required for it — SendAsync already POSTs a
    form to `v0/{slug}` under Basic auth, and SendZstdJsonLinesAsync was built in #35 for these
    endpoints and has no caller in M3.

    Rejected: hanging the reference subclients off HistoricalClient itself. ROADMAP.md §6 decided a
    separate client and a separate package, and upstream agrees.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../DatabentoDotNet.Historical/DatabentoDotNet.Historical.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    No zstd here, and that is a decision rather than an omission.

    Every reference `get_range` returns a zstd-framed JSONL body, so this package obviously needs
    that decoding — it just does not need to *own* it. The reader lives on HistoricalClient
    (ReadZstdJsonLinesAsync / SendZstdJsonLinesAsync), which is where upstream puts it too:
    handle_zstd_jsonl_response is defined in historical/client.rs:212 even though only reference/
    calls it. So this csproj carries neither ZstdSharp.Port nor a second linked copy of
    Internal/ZstdDecompressor.cs, and CLAUDE.md's rule that every zstd call goes through that one
    file stays literally true with one fewer copy of it in the repo, not one more.

    NodaTime is absent for the same shape of reason: nothing in this project's public API names one
    of its types yet. The reference range (#49) is the first thing that will, and it names the
    package then. It reaches consumers transitively through the codec regardless.
  -->

</Project>
